Labelling observations with geom_text()
geom_text() is geom_point() plus a label aesthetic. Build a small data frame of just the things you want labelled:

label_info &lt;- mpg |&gt;
  group_by(drv) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(displ)) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 1) |&gt;
  mutate(
    drive_type = case_when(
      drv == "f" ~ "front-wheel drive",
      drv == "r" ~ "rear-wheel drive",
      drv == "4" ~ "4-wheel drive"
    )
  ) |&gt;
  select(displ, hwy, drv, drive_type)

label_info

# A tibble: 3 × 4
# Groups:   drv [3]
  displ   hwy drv   drive_type       
  &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;            
1   6.5    17 4     4-wheel drive    
2   5.3    25 f     front-wheel drive
3   7      24 r     rear-wheel drive

Overriding the stat
When you already have the counts, switch to the identity stat (or use geom_col()):

diamonds |&gt;
  count(cut) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = cut, y = n)) +
  geom_bar(stat = "identity")

The extended template
ggplot(data = &lt;DATA&gt;) +
  &lt;GEOM_FUNCTION&gt;(
     mapping = aes(&lt;MAPPINGS&gt;),
     stat = &lt;STAT&gt;,
     position = &lt;POSITION&gt;
  ) +
  &lt;COORDINATE_FUNCTION&gt; +
  &lt;FACET_FUNCTION&gt;
Seven slots. In practice you fill in three — data, mappings, geom — because ggplot2 supplies sensible defaults for the rest.

Tidy data: benefits are stuck in wide format

survey_2017_raw |&gt;
  select(respondent_id, starts_with("benefit_")) |&gt;
  head()








Function


One respondent has a single true preference, but right now it’s scattered across six separate columns. Before we can do anything useful with it, we need to reshape it with pivot_longer().

Tidy data: pivot_longer()

benefits_2017_long &lt;- survey_2017_raw |&gt;
  pivot_longer(
    cols = starts_with("benefit_"),
    names_to = "benefit_type",
    values_to = "response"
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(chosen = response %in% c("Yes", "Y", "yes")) |&gt;
  group_by(respondent_id) |&gt;
  summarize(
    benefit_preference = if (any(chosen)) benefit_type[chosen][1] else NA_character_,
    .groups = "drop"
  )

survey_2017_clean &lt;- survey_2017_raw |&gt;
  select(-starts_with("benefit_")) |&gt;
  left_join(benefits_2017_long, by = "respondent_id")

Back to the Income Example







Solution


Before income can be used in any calculation, the income data needs to be transformed into a real number.





clean_income &lt;- function(x) {
  x &lt;- str_trim(x)
  is_k &lt;- str_detect(x, "K$")
  numeric_part &lt;- x |&gt;
    str_remove_all("[$,K]") |&gt;
    as.numeric()
  if_else(is_k, numeric_part * 1000, numeric_part)
}

survey_2017_clean &lt;- survey_2017_clean |&gt;
  mutate(income = clean_income(income))

survey_2022_clean &lt;- survey_2022_clean |&gt;
  mutate(income = clean_income(income))
